  "notes": "Royal letters include a collective Cistercian appeal. Monastic common property is not treated as a proposal to collectivize society. Protecting Jews from violence did not imply equal legal status. No modern national martyr cult, universal interest ban, or specific apostasy penalty is inferred. The Wendish appeal is recorded at low confidence because interpretations differ. The source collection’s editorial commentary is not his speech. Remaining null statements reviewed across all ten themes; monastic asceticism and forest imagery do not establish modern environmental or technology policies.\n\nMerged with an independent claude-sonnet-5-5 audit: 8 answers taken from it, 21 agreed, 2 conflicts settled by the integrator.\n\nclaude-sonnet-5-5 notes: Independent second pass from the Mabillon/Eales English translation of his letters and sermons, On Consideration and the New Knighthood (full text downloaded and searched by theme), not from the first audit's answers. Themes searched: economy (usury, property, charity, trade, tolls, labour), government (kings, the Roman republic, appeals, exemptions, elections), world (crusades, Jews, wars between Christian princes), society (marriage, heresy, poverty, dress), liberty, culture, ecology and technology. Economy, ecology, technology and most of culture are null: his letters and treatises do not speak to them. Answers on Church governance (bishops, exemptions) rest on his words about the Pope and the Roman curia. The Wendish letter's alternative of reforming the pagans' worship or destroying the nation is read as ambiguous; the crusade answers rest on his own appeals, not on what armies later did.",
